            <ArticleTitle>Metastatic mammary carcinoma to the orbit masquerading as maxillary sinusitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>34-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2500/ar.2016.7.0143</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">We report on a case of isolated metastatic breast cancer to the medial rectus muscle. This entity is exceedingly rare.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 44-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presented with unilateral maxillary symptoms and was treated for sinusitis. Over time, she developed ocular pain, diplopia, blurred vision and eventually complete adduction deficit.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">T1-weighted magnetic resonance imaging revealed a medial rectus lesion. Biopsy via transnasal transorbital endoscopic approach revealed metastatic mammary carcinoma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Metastatic disease to the orbit should be considered in the differential diagnosis of refractory maxillary sinus pain in patients with a known underlying malignancy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
